# curate-skills deploy settings — template.
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/curate && cp .env.template ~/.config/curate/.env && chmod 600 ~/.config/curate/.env
#
# Then fill in ~/.config/curate/.env with an editor (e.g. `open -e ~/.config/curate/.env` or `nano …`).
# Write each value right after the `=`: no spaces around it, no quotes needed.
# Lines marked SECRET hold credentials: type or paste them only into this file, in your editor.
# Never paste them into a Claude Code session or any chat.
#
# Only the deploy alias reads this file, in a subshell (see the README, "Deploy"); don't `source`
# it in your login shell. Check it with /curate-skills:curate-doctor --deploy --online.
#
# The three SECRET values must ALSO be set as GitHub Actions secrets on your action repo, under the
# same names (the workflows read them at run time): repo → Settings → Secrets and variables →
# Actions → New repository secret, or in a terminal `gh secret set GH_PAT -R <owner>/<repo>`
# (it prompts for the value), likewise S3_AccessKey and S3_SecretKey.

# ── GitHub ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Your GitHub user (or organisation) name: the <owner> in github.com/<owner>/<repo>.
# Example format: alice-smith
FAASR_GH_USERNAME=

# Your own action repo: FaaSr runs each function as a GitHub Actions workflow there, and deploy
# commits the workflow files and function code into it. The repo NAME only, not owner/repo.
# Create it at https://github.com/new with "Add a README" ticked (it needs a first commit).
# Example format: faasr-actions
FAASR_ACTION_REPO=

# The action repo's default branch (shown on the repo page). Empty means main.
# Example format: main
FAASR_ACTION_BRANCH=

# SECRET. A CLASSIC GitHub personal access token with scopes repo and workflow.
# Create it: GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens → Tokens (classic)
# → Generate new token (classic); tick "repo" and "workflow". (Fine-grained tokens are not
# recommended: doctor can't check their write access.)
# Example format: ghp_ followed by 36 letters and digits
GH_PAT=

# ── S3 storage (data passed between the workflow's steps) ───────────────────

# The S3 endpoint URL. AWS: https://s3.amazonaws.com or https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com.
# MinIO or another S3-compatible service: its https URL.
# Example format: https://s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com
FAASR_S3_ENDPOINT=

# The bucket name only: no s3://, no folder. AWS console: S3 → Buckets.
# Example format: my-faasr-data
FAASR_S3_BUCKET=

# The bucket's region. AWS console: the bucket's "AWS Region" column. MinIO: often us-east-1.
# Example format: us-east-1
FAASR_S3_REGION=

# SECRET. Access key ID of a key pair that can read and write the bucket.
# AWS: IAM → Users → <user> → Security credentials → Create access key.
# Example format: AKIA followed by 16 capital letters and digits
S3_AccessKey=

# SECRET. The secret access key of that key pair (shown once, when the key is created).
# Example format: 40 letters, digits, / and +
S3_SecretKey=

# ── Optional (leave empty for the defaults) ─────────────────────────────────

# Suffix added to every deployed name, so a curate-skills deployment never overwrites another
# one in the same repo or bucket. Empty means -cs. Letters, digits, - and _ only.
# Example format: -cs-alice
CURATE_DEPLOY_NAME_SUFFIX=

# How long curate-monitor watches a deployed run before stopping with a resume command.
# Empty means 10. Example format: 20
CURATE_MONITOR_MINUTES=
